      "text": "To solve the inequality \\(\\sin(x+y) \\leq \\sin(x) + \\sin(y)\\) for all \\(x \\in [0, \\pi]\\) and \\(y \\in [0, \\pi]\\), we will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Isolate the sine functions:**\n   \\[\n   \\sin(x+y) - \\sin(x) \\leq \\sin(y)\n   \\]\n\n2. **Use the sine addition formula:**\n   \\[\n   \\sin(x+y) = \\sin(x)\\cos(y) + \\cos(x)\\sin(y)\n   \\]\n   Substituting this into the inequality, we get:\n   \\[\n   \\sin(x)\\cos(y) + \\cos(x)\\sin(y) - \\sin(x) \\leq \\sin(y)\n   \\]\n\n3. **Rearrange the terms:**\n   \\[\n   \\sin(x)\\cos(y) + \\cos(x)\\sin(y) - \\sin(x) - \\sin(y) \\leq 0\n   \\]\n\n4. **Factor out common terms:**\n   \\[\n   \\sin(x)(\\cos(y) - 1) + \\sin(y)(\\cos(x)) - \\sin(x) \\leq 0\n   \\]\n\n5. **Combine like terms:**\n   \\[\n   \\sin(x)(\\cos(y) - 1 + 1) + \\sin(y)(\\cos(x)) \\leq 0\n   \\]\n   Simplifying further:\n   \\[\n   \\sin(x)\\cos(y) + \\sin(y)\\cos(x) \\leq 0\n   \\]\n\n6. **Use the product-to-sum identities:**\n   Note that \\(\\sin(x)\\cos(y) + \\sin(y)\\cos(x) = \\sin(x+y)\\). Thus, the inequality becomes:\n   \\[\n   \\sin(x+y) \\leq 0\n   \\]\n\n7. **Analyze the sine function:**\n   The sine function \\(\\sin(x+y)\\) is non-positive when \\(x+y \\in [\\pi n, \\pi (n+1)]\\) for any integer \\(n\\). Since \\(y \\in [0, \\pi]\\), the only relevant interval is when \\(x+y \\geq \\pi\\).\n\n8. **Find the possible values of \\(x\\):**\n   If \\(x+y \\geq \\pi\\), then \\(x \\geq \\pi - y\\). Since \\(y \\in [0, \\pi]\\), the maximum value for \\(x\\) is \\(\\pi\\). Therefore, \\(x \\in [\\pi - y, \\pi]\\).\n\n9. **Conclusion:**\n   For the inequality \\(\\sin(x+y) \\leq \\sin(x) + \\sin(y)\\) to hold for all \\(x \\in [0, \\pi]\\) and \\(y \\in [0, \\pi]\\), it must be true that \\(x \\geq \\pi - y\\). This condition holds for all \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) in their respective intervals, indicating that the inequality holds for the entire interval.\n\nThus, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{[0, \\pi]}\\).",
