      "text": "To find all \\( y \\in [0, \\pi] \\) such that the inequality\n\n\\[\n\\sin(x+y) \\leq \\sin(x) + \\sin(y)\n\\]\n\nholds for all \\( x \\in [0, \\pi] \\), we will analyze the given inequality step by step.\n\nFirst, we use the angle addition formula for sine:\n\n\\[\n\\sin(x+y) = \\sin x \\cos y + \\cos x \\sin y\n\\]\n\nThus, the inequality becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\sin x \\cos y + \\cos x \\sin y \\leq \\sin x + \\sin y\n\\]\n\nRearrange terms to isolate \\(\\sin x\\) and \\(\\sin y\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sin x \\cos y - \\sin x \\leq \\sin y - \\cos x \\sin y\n\\]\n\nFactor out common terms:\n\n\\[\n\\sin x (\\cos y - 1) \\leq \\sin y (1 - \\cos x)\n\\]\n\nNext, we require this inequality to hold for all \\( x \\in [0, \\pi] \\). To gain insight, consider specific values of \\( x \\) and analyze the resulting inequalities.\n\n### Step 1: Consider \\( x = 0 \\)\n\nFor \\( x = 0 \\), the inequality simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\sin y \\cos 0 + \\cos 0 \\sin y \\leq \\sin 0 + \\sin y\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\cos 0 = 1\\) and \\(\\sin 0 = 0\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\sin y \\cdot 1 + 1 \\cdot \\sin y \\leq 0 + \\sin y\n\\]\n\nSimplifying:\n\n\\[\n\\sin y + \\sin y \\leq \\sin y\n\\]\n\nThis is always true for any \\( y \\in [0, \\pi] \\).\n\n### Step 2: Consider \\( x = \\pi \\)\n\nFor \\( x = \\pi \\), the inequality becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\sin \\pi \\cos y + \\cos \\pi \\sin y \\leq \\sin \\pi + \\sin y\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\sin \\pi = 0\\) and \\(\\cos \\pi = -1\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n0 \\cdot \\cos y - 1 \\cdot \\sin y \\leq 0 + \\sin y\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n-\\sin y \\leq \\sin y\n\\]\n\nThis inequality is only true if \\(\\sin y \\leq 0\\).\n\n### Step 3: Analyze \\(\\sin y \\leq 0\\) for \\( y \\in [0, \\pi] \\)\n\nThe sine function is non-positive in the interval \\( [0, \\pi] \\). From our previous step, we know \\(\\sin y \\leq 0\\) for \\( y \\in [0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}] \\).\n\n### Step 4: Verify \\( y \\in [0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}] \\)\n\nTo ensure that \\( y \\in [0, \\pi] \\) covers all possible scenarios, we need to check the endpoint \\( y = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\):\n\nFor \\( y = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\):\n\n\\[\n\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{2} = 1\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\sin x + \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{2} = \\sin x + 1\n\\]\n\nWe need:\n\n\\[\n\\sin x \\cos \\frac{\\pi}{2} + \\cos x \\sin \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\leq \\sin x + 1\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\cos \\frac{\\pi}{2} = 0\\) and \\(\\sin \\frac{\\pi}{2} = 1\\), the left-hand side becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\sin x \\cdot 0 + \\cos x \\cdot 1 = \\cos x\n\\]\n\nThus, we need:\n\n\\[\n\\cos x \\leq \\sin x + 1\n\\]\n\nThis inequality is always true for \\( x \\in [0, \\pi] \\), confirming that \\( y = \\frac{\\pi}{2} \\) satisfies the original inequality.\n\n### Conclusion\n\nCombining the results from steps 1-3, we conclude that the inequality holds for all \\( y \\in [0, \\pi] \\) given by:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{[0, \\frac{\\pi}{2}]}\n\\]",
