      "text": "Any angle satisfies $\\sin(x+y) = \\sin(x) + \\sin(y)$. This is equivalent to $\\sin(x)=\\sin(y)$. Since both $0$ and $\\pi$ are solutions, $\\boxed{y=0, y=\\pi}, \\boxed{[0.\\pi]}$.\n\nSolution using $x_0$ between $0$ and $\\pi$\nTherefore two cases exist. $|x_0|<\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ or $|x_0|>\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\nIf $|x_0|<\\frac{\\pi}{2}$, then $\\forall x$,\n$\\sin(x)>\\sin(x_0)$\n$\\sin(y)>\\lvert\\sin(x_0)\\rvert=\\sin(x_0)$\n$\\sin(x+y)>\\sin(x)+\\sin(y)$\nIf $|x_0|>\\frac{\\pi}{2}$, then $\\forall x$,\n$\\sin(x)<\\sin(x_0)$\n$\\sin(y)<-\\sin(x_0)=\\sin(x_0)$\n$\\sin(x+y)<\\sin(x)+\\sin(y)$\nSo $\\boxed{y=0, y=\\pi}$.\n\nSolution through $x_0 = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$\nAny angle in this domain satisfies $\\sin(x+y) = -\\cos(y)$\nso we need $\\sin(x) + \\sin(y) \\geq -\\cos(y) \\Rightarrow \\cos(y) - \\sin(y) \\geq 0 \\Rightarrow y^2-y-1 \\geq 0 \\Rightarrow y=0$\ndrawing a graph for the answer $\\boxed{[0,\\pi]}$.\n\nSo $\\forall x \\in \\mathbb{R}$, $\\sin x = s$, hence there is always at least one $y = y'$ s.t. $\\sin'(x+y') = s$;\n\nTrying $\\forall x \\in [0,\\pi]$, for $s = 0$;\n\nLooking at the graph, the smallest value of $\\sin(x)$ is $\\sin 0 = \\sin x$, hence $y = 0$ must hold true.\n\nLooking at the graph, the largest values of $\\sin(x)$ is $\\sin \\pi = -\\sin x$, hence $\\sin(\\pi + y) = -\\sin y$ must hold true giving us $\\boxed{y = \\pi}$.\n\nNow, by the S.A. rule\n\n$\\forall  x \\in [0,\\pi],$ $\\sin{x + y} \\leq \\sin{x} + \\sin{y}$ iff $\\cos{y} \\leq 1$ for $0 \\leq y \\leq \\pi$; here, $\\cos{y}$ is maximal in $\\sin{y}$, 0 being the max point in $[0,\\pi]$\n\nSo, $y = 0$ and $y = \\pi \\rightarrow \\{0, \\pi\\}$\n\n[asy]\nimport graph;\nreal f(real x,real y)\n{\n return sin(x+y) - sin(x) - sin(y);\n}\nreal xmin = -2 * atan(1), xmax = 0.966 + 2 * atan(1), ymin = -0.95 + -atan(1), ymax = 0.09 + -atan(1);\nsize(5cm);\ndraw((xmin, f(xmin, 0))--(xmax, f(xmax, 0)),red);\ndraw((xmin,`$\\pi$'`)--(xmax,`$\\pi$'`),red); draw((xmin,`$0$'`)--(xmax,`$0$'`),red); draw((xmin,f(xmin,0))--(xmax,f(xmax,0)));  xaxis(\"${0, \\pi}$\",-8.33333333333333,3,HenlyAngle,LeftReverse); yaxis(\"${-\\pi, \\pi}$\",-2.45,-1.31,4,RadinAngle,Left); dot((0,-0.966)); dot((-2028.0/sqrt(11),2)); dot((-2028.0/sqrt(11),2.0));  label(\"$\\sin(x+y) \\leq \\sin x + \\sin y$\",(-10,0),0); [/asy].\nActually, I forgot the condition that $y$ has to be between $0$ and $\\pi.$\nWe have $\\sin y - \\sin x \\geq 0$ implies $\\cos x \\cos y \\geq 1$.  However, $\\cos x \\leq 1$, so $\\cos x \\cos y \\leq \\cos y \\Rightarrow \\cos y \\geq 1$ is a contradiction.  So, $0 < y < \\pi$.  The solution is $\\boxed{\\{0, \\pi \\}}$.",
